    <div class="highlight-box light"><h2>Box A: Insights into recent increases in food prices in Ireland</h2><p><strong>by Katie Bourke and Gabriel Arce Alfaro<sup><a href="#footnote-10" id="footnote-ref-10" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[9]</a></sup></strong></p><p>Recent data on Irish inflation indicate broadly stable prices arising in the first half of 2025, with a year-on-year headline inflation rate of 1.6 per cent in July. However, food prices have risen significantly, recording a 4.2 per cent increase&mdash;more than double the headline rate (Figure 1). Food inflation accounted for over 40 per cent of July&rsquo;s overall price change, driven primarily by a few key products, particularly meat. This analysis explores the factors behind these increases.<br /></p><hr /><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Meat accounts for the largest share of food inflation in recent months</h3><h4>Figure 1: Food inflation decomposition</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-a-figure-1.png?sfvrsn=c9686e1a_1" height="670" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box A Figure 1" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="82769" /></figure><p><small>Source: CSO data and Central Bank of Ireland calculations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-a1.csv?sfvrsn=67756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 6.15KB)</a></small></p><p>The rise in food prices is concentrated in three categories: meat, oils and fats, and confectionery (Table 1). Among these, meat has the largest weight in the food inflation basket at 15.6 per cent, compared to 3 per cent for oils and fats and 9.4 per cent for confectionery.&nbsp;</p><h3 class="H3exclude">Meat, butter and chocolate prices have increased sharply in 2025</h3><h4>Table 1. Year-on-Year growth rate of food prices in Ireland</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/table-1-box-a.png?sfvrsn=df766e1a_1" height="233" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Table 1 Box A" width="1057" alt="See alternative description in notes below." sf-size="48914" /><p><small>Accessibility: This table shows that the rise in food prices up to July 2025 is concentrated in three categories: meat, oils and fats, and confectionery.</small></p><p>Going further, the data indicate that very specific items in these three categories are driving overall food price inflation. Within oils and fats, the rise has been primarily driven by butter, which recorded an average price increase of 15.2 per cent in 2025, due to reduced milk supply and constrained production in Europe alongside extreme weather affecting feed availability for herds. In the sugar, jam, honey, chocolate and confectionery category, the upward pressure has been concentrated in chocolate, with average increase of 15.1 per cent with respect to last year&rsquo;s prices. This has arisen with cocoa prices exceeding $12,000 per tonne as harvests in West Africa have been poor due to storms, droughts and crop diseases.</p><p>However, meat remains the largest contributor to food inflation, accounting for 1.4 percentage points of the 4.6 per cent year-on-year increase in food prices in July&nbsp;(Figure 1). Within the meat category, the rise is concentrated in beef and veal, which saw a 23 per cent year-on-year price increase. In contrast, pork prices rose by just 0.8 per cent, and poultry by 4.2 per cent. </p><p>Ireland is currently the fifth largest world exporter of beef and veal, and similar consumer price trends for these products can be seen in the UK, which is Ireland&rsquo;s top beef export market, to where currently over 40 per cent of Irish beef produce is exported (Figure 2). After the UK, Ireland&rsquo;s next largest export markets are France and the Netherlands, accounting for roughly 13 and 9 per cent of Ireland&rsquo;s beef export volume, respectively. Nineteen per cent of exports are sold to other markets within the EU. These countries have all seen a rise in consumer meat prices since the beginning of 2025, where the latest beef and veal prices for the UK and the Netherlands are over 20 per cent higher year-on-year, while data for France remain elevated but more modest. This raises the question: what explains the increase in consumer beef prices in Ireland that is also being observed in the UK and some EU countries? </p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Recent consumer price increases in Beef and Veal are also evident in other European countries</h3><h4>Figure 2: Beef and Veal Prices (Year-on-Year % change)</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-a-figure-2.png?sfvrsn=f686e1a_1" height="560" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box A Figure 2" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="38361" /></figure><p><small>Source: European Commission. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-a2.csv?sfvrsn=47756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 0.48KB)</a></small></p><p>This increase in beef and veal prices does not appear to be the result of a pass-through of input prices, which have stabilised since mid-2023 (Figure 3). While fertiliser prices increased by 10 per cent year-on-year in June, this is insufficient to explain the 20.7 per cent rise in farm gate output prices for beef, driven by a nearly 50 per cent increase in cattle prices. This suggests that the current surge instead reflects tight external supply and demand pressures rather than the pass-through of input prices at the farm gate level.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Agricultural output prices have increased at a faster pace than input prices since early 2024</h3><h4>Figure 3: Agricultural Input and Output Prices Index in Ireland</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-a-figure-3.png?sfvrsn=6f686e1a_1" height="648" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box A Figure 3" width="1002" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="20213" /></figure><p><small>Source: CSO. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-a3.csv?sfvrsn=b8766e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 1.59KB)</a></small></p><p>Looking at the supply side, EU beef and veal production is down by 3.6 per cent across the bloc since the beginning of 2025. Supply contractions are widespread, with notable declines from major producers as well as some of Ireland&rsquo;s top export markets such as the Netherlands, France and Germany (Figure 4). While production in Ireland is relatively stable, live exports from Ireland have increased, and were up by 20 per cent year-on-year by mid-2025.The UK shows a very similar profile, with overall supply down by 4 per cent in the first half of the year when compared to 2024. This supply contraction is part of a longer-term trend, with the EU breeding herd shrinking by 1 to 3 per cent annually since 2017.<sup><a href="#footnote-11" id="footnote-ref-11" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[10]</a></sup> Despite rising prices, demand for beef has been relatively inelastic, both domestically and in key export markets. </p><p>The imbalance between tight supply and steady demand has driven up live animal and carcass prices across the EU and UK, which, in turn, has pushed up farm gate prices in Ireland.<sup><a href="#footnote-12" id="footnote-ref-12" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[11]</a></sup> It is this increase in cattle and carcass prices at farm gate level &ndash; which reflects the interplay between demand and supply developments in Europe &ndash; that appears to be the main factor accounting for the rise in consumer prices for meat in Ireland over recent months. The Industrial Producer Price Index published by the CSO measures the change over time in the selling prices received by domestic producers of goods and services. Consistent with the rise in farm gate prices, domestic producer prices for meat and meat products have increased over the first seven months of the year. In July, producer prices for meat and meat products were 6.7 per cent higher than in July 2024.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">EU Beef and Veal supply tightens across Europe</h3><h4>Figure 4: Year-on-Year change in Beef and Veal Production up to May 2025</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-a-figure-4.png?sfvrsn=48686e1a_1" height="604" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box A Figure 4" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below." sf-size="18089" /></figure><p><small>Source: European Commision. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-a4.csv?sfvrsn=a2766e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 0.31KB)</a></small></p><p>Looking ahead, the demand and supply factors behind the rise in meat prices in 2025 appear structural in nature, implying that prices are expected to remain at elevated levels but the rapid increases observed over recent months are unlikely to persist. The latest OECD outlook points to a moderation in global beef prices over the medium term influenced by slowing demand, reduced real feed costs and continuous productivity improvements.<sup><a href="#footnote-13" id="footnote-ref-13" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[12]</a></sup> Should this occur, it implies that inflation in beef prices at the retail level in Ireland should decline to reflect the stable (though higher) farm gate price level for cattle carcasses in the EU. More broadly, the technical assumptions on international food commodity prices underpinning the overall forecasts for food inflation in the <em>Bulletin </em>envisage a slowdown in the pace of price growth out to 2027. While recent spikes in food price inflation are expected to ease, the outlook remains sensitive to unexpected supply and demand shocks affecting specific products. </p></div>
    
   

    
    <div class="highlight-box light"><h2>Box B: The pass-through of expected house price changes to inflation expectations</h2><p><strong>by Gabriel Arce Alfaro and Prachi Srivastava<sup><a href="#footnote-14" id="footnote-ref-14" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[13]</a></sup></strong></p><p>Expectations about the economy&rsquo;s future performance play a pivotal role in macroeconomics, particularly for central banks seeking to understand how households form inflation expectations. Households&rsquo; beliefs about future inflation influence current price levels through consumption-savings decisions and wage-setting, thereby affecting inflation dynamics. Evidence suggests that expectations or experiences of house prices significantly shape households&rsquo; inflation expectations, as housing is both a consumption good and a key asset. Expectations about house prices, therefore, influence broader views on price developments (<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780128229279000124" data-sf-ec-immutable="">D&rsquo;Acunto et al (2023)</a> and <a href="https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:c8772ec3-f1b7-4357-bd47-eb7fdd323861/files/sh989r524z" data-sf-ec-immutable="">Dhamija V. et al (2025)</a>).</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Housing inflation in Ireland has remained above EU average in recent years</h3><h4>Figure 1: Year-on-year price changes</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-1.png?sfvrsn=a1696e1a_1" height="873" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 1" width="1481" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="34992" /></figure><p><small>Source: Eurostat and Central Bank calculations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b1.csv?sfvrsn=dc756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 0.43KB)</a></small></p><p>In Ireland, house prices and rental inflation have consistently outpaced the euro area average (Figure 1). The divergence in housing-related inflation and headline HICP inflation has also been larger in Ireland, contrasting with the euro area average, where the two have for the most part remained aligned. This divergence highlights the importance of examining not only actual housing market developments but also households&rsquo; expectations of future house prices. Housing&rsquo;s significant role in household budgets means expected house prices can influence inflation expectations through cost-of-living and wealth channels. The strength of this pass-through may vary by tenure group: homeowners may view rising house prices as wealth gains, while renters associate them with higher future rents and living costs. Understanding these differences is crucial for analysing how housing market expectations shape inflation dynamics in Ireland.</p><p>Ireland has experienced a substantial increase in house prices in recent years. According to the CSO Residential Property Index, national house price inflation rose to 8.3 per cent year-on-year in 2025Q1, up from 5.4 per cent in 2024Q1, while HICP inflation was just 1.9 per cent over the same period. Although the consumer price index reflects only consumption-related housing aspects (e.g., rents, housing services, utilities), a long-run relationship between house prices and rents is well established. Thus, changes in house prices indirectly affect consumer price inflation through their impact on rents. </p><p>This raises a key question: how do households&rsquo; expectations of housing market developments, particularly house prices, shape their overall inflation expectations? Using the ECB Consumer Expectation Survey (CES), we examine the relationship between expected house price developments and inflation expectations. The literature in this area highlights the long-run relationship between housing market dynamics and inflation, with expected house prices influencing inflation expectations through two channels. First, households may anticipate that higher house prices will lead to future upward adjustments to rents. Secondly, house prices affect both household expenses and wealth, shaping broader inflation expectations.</p><p>Housing services costs&rsquo; weight in household budgets further underscores the relevance of this link. Housing and related services account for 15&ndash;18 per cent of the consumer basket in both Ireland and the EU. <sup><a href="#footnote-15" id="footnote-ref-15" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[14]</a></sup> However, in Ireland, actual rents comprise 50&ndash;60 per cent of total housing services expenditure, about 20 percentage points higher than the EU average. This higher share is accompanied by elevated rent inflation, which has exceeded headline inflation since 2018. For instance, while EU housing services inflation peaked at 24 per cent in 2022, Ireland&rsquo;s peak was significantly higher at 31.8 per cent in October 2022.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude" id="unique-id-1">Expenditure on actual rents in Ireland is 20 percentage points higher than the EU average</h3><h4>Figure 2: Expenditure Share</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-2.png?sfvrsn=8a696e1a_1" height="563" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 2" width="902" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="23807" /></figure><p><small>Source: Eurostat and CSO. Note: The vertical axis measures the weight of categories in the overall HICP which is out of 1000 parts. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b2.csv?sfvrsn=d2756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format (CSV 0.38KB)</a></small></p><p>Irish households also report higher inflation expectations than the EU average. During the inflation surge in mid-2022, Irish households&rsquo; one-year-ahead inflation expectations exceeded the EU average by 4 per cent (Figure 3). Renters in Ireland consistently expect higher inflation than homeowners, by approximately 1.5 per cent, reflecting the salience of housing costs. In contrast, at the EU level, renters, outright owners, and mortgagors report similar inflation expectations.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Renters report higher inflation expectations on average in Ireland</h3><h4>Figure 3: Inflation expectations</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-3.png?sfvrsn=ea696e1a_1" height="639" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 3" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="49718" /></figure><p><small>Source: Consumer Expectation Survey data and Central Bank of Ireland calculations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b3.csv?sfvrsn=c0756e1a_1">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 4.41KB)</a></small></p><p>A similar pattern emerges for expected house prices. In Ireland, renters anticipate higher future house prices than homeowners and mortgagors, by roughly 2 per cent between 2022 and mid-2024 (Figure 4). At the EU level, expected house prices are broadly aligned across tenure groups. These differences highlight the importance of housing costs for renters in Ireland.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Irish average house price expectations are higher among renters</h3><h4>Figure 4: House Price Expectations</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-4.png?sfvrsn=d7696e1a_1" height="636" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 4" width="1002" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="55299" /></figure><p><small>Source: Consumer Expectation Survey data and Central Bank of Ireland calculations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b4.csv?sfvrsn=3c756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 4.44KB)</a><br />Note: CES data for Ireland is available from April 2022.</small></p><h3 class="H3exclude">What do survey data tell us about the relationship?</h3><p>Using the CES survey data, we measure how much house price expectations pass through to overall inflation expectations. We find that when households anticipate higher house prices over the next 12 months, they also tend to raise their short-term inflation expectations. This relationship holds across different groups of households and is stronger in Ireland than in the euro area average (Figure 5). A linear regression of one-year-ahead house price and inflation expectations, controlling for individual characteristics (e.g., demographics, macroeconomic expectations, region, and time fixed effects), estimates the pass-through at 0.29&ndash;0.35 percentage points for the EU (including and excluding Ireland) and Ireland. The pass-through in Ireland exceeds the EU average by 0.07 percentage points, a statistically significant difference. </p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Housing stands out as more salient for Irish households&rsquo; inflation expectations</h3><h4>Figure 5: Pass-through of house prices to inflation expectations </h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-5.png?sfvrsn=30696e1a_1" height="649" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 5" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below" sf-size="4929" /></figure><p><small>Source: Consumer Expectations Survey and Central Bank of Ireland estimations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b5.csv?sfvrsn=14756e1a_1" target="_blank">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 0.38KB)</a> Note: The numbers represents the point estimate from our linear regressions and whiskers represents the 95 per cent confidence interval around the estimate. </small></p><p>Building on the differences observed across tenure groups, we analyse the effects further through the perspective of housing tenure. More specifically, we compare the differences between homeowners (outright owners and mortgagors) and renters. Figure 6 illustrates how the pass-through of expected house prices to inflation expectations differs across tenure groups. In both categories, the pass-through in Ireland exceeds the EU average by more than 0.06 percentage points. In Ireland, renters show a 0.05 percentage point higher pass-through compared to homeowners; however, given the shorter sample available for Ireland, the distinction between these two subgroups is less pronounced due to greater estimation uncertainty. These differences point at the potential role of tenure status in shaping how housing dynamics influence overall expected inflation.</p><figure><h3 class="H3exclude">Renters show higher pass-through to inflation expectations</h3><h4>Figure 6: Pass-through of expected house prices to inflation expectations by house tenure</h4><img sf-image-responsive="true" src="/images/default-source/publication-images/quarterly-bulletin/quarterly-bulletin-3-2025-charts/box-b-figure-6.png?sfvrsn=1d696e1a_1" height="588" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" title="Box B Figure 6" width="1003" alt="Get the data in accessible format in notes below." sf-size="4820" /></figure><p><small>Source: Consumer Expectations Survey and Central Bank of Ireland estimations. <a href="/docs/default-source/publications/quarterly-bulletins/quarterly-bulletin-3-csvs/figure-b6.csv?sfvrsn=2756e1a_1">Chart data in accessible format. (CSV 0.37KB)</a><br />Note: The numbers represents the point estimate from our linear regressions and whiskers represents the 95 per cent confidence interval around the estimate. </small></p><p>Recent literature has focused on how price changes in salient categories like food and energy shape inflation expectations. The analysis here highlights the similarly important role of housing, particularly in Ireland. The findings underscore how housing market dynamics, especially expected house prices and tenure group differences, significantly influence inflation expectations. Given the higher weight of housing costs in Irish household budgets compared to the EU average, housing market dynamics exert a stronger influence on expected inflation in Ireland. Monitoring expected house prices is important, as they can amplify inflation through the expectations channel, influencing consumption-savings decisions and short-term price dynamics.</p></div>
    
   

    
    <h2 id="unique-id-36">An Timpeallacht Gheilleagrach</h2><p class="intro">T&aacute; gn&oacute;tha&iacute;, teaghlaigh agus lucht cinnteoireachta in &Eacute;irinn ag dul in oiri&uacute;int don timpeallacht athraithe i bhfianaise caidreamh eacnama&iacute;och trasatlantach nua at&aacute; ag teacht chun cinn. C&eacute; nach bhfuil an t-ionchas eacnama&iacute;och chomh fabhrach agus a bheadh mura mbeadh na taraif&iacute; tugtha isteach ag na St&aacute;it Aontaithe, t&aacute; na r&aacute;ta&iacute; taraife &eacute;ifeachtacha at&aacute; i bhfeidhm faoi l&aacute;thair maidir le tr&aacute;d&aacute;il AE-SA ag an gceann &iacute;ochtarach den raon leathan tortha&iacute; ab fh&eacute;ideartha ag t&uacute;s na bliana. C&eacute; go bhfuil laghd&uacute; tagtha ar an &eacute;iginnteacht beartais, t&aacute; an &eacute;iginnteacht sin f&oacute;s &aacute;bhartha ar bhonn int&iacute;re agus domhanda. Meastar go maol&oacute;idh an fuinneamh at&aacute; taobh thiar den fh&aacute;s eacnama&iacute;och leis an athr&uacute; seo chuig timpeallacht sheachtrach nua. Ag an am c&eacute;anna, t&aacute; srianta &oacute; thaobh an tsol&aacute;thair de f&oacute;s le feice&aacute;il ar an bhf&aacute;s inbhuanaithe sa gheilleagar int&iacute;re. Beidh iarracht chomhbheartaithe beartais de dh&iacute;th chun dul i ngleic leo. Tr&iacute; amhlaidh a dh&eacute;anamh, i dteannta dinimiceas an ghn&oacute; &aacute;iti&uacute;il a choth&uacute; agus l&aacute;nph&aacute;irti&uacute; <em>de facto</em> na h&Eacute;ireann i margadh aonair AE, neart&oacute;far an geilleagar i leith timpeallacht gheopholaiti&uacute;il n&iacute;os scoilte thar an me&aacute;nt&eacute;arma agus cothabh&aacute;ilfear tarraingteacht an gheilleagair don infheist&iacute;ocht dh&iacute;reach choigr&iacute;che.</p><p>I neart OTI pr&iacute;omha sa ch&eacute;ad leath de 2025, l&eacute;ir&iacute;tear go p&aacute;irteach freagairt na bhfiontar iln&aacute;isi&uacute;nta (FINanna) ar tharaif&iacute; ionchasacha SA le t&uacute;s-ual&uacute; onnmhair&iacute;. Mar a cuireadh in i&uacute;l san <em>Fhaisn&eacute;is R&aacute;ithi&uacute;il</em> roimhe seo, &aacute;fach, bh&iacute; bunspreagadh d&rsquo;onnmhair&iacute; ag teacht go h&aacute;irithe &oacute;n tionscal leathnaitheach c&oacute;gais&iacute;ochta freisin, mar aon leis an bhf&aacute;s ar onnmhair&iacute; neamhch&oacute;gais&iacute;ochta. Ag f&eacute;achaint romhainn, beidh tionchar ar phr&iacute;omhth&aacute;scair&iacute; gheilleagar na h&Eacute;ireann ag an gcaoi ina n-iompr&oacute;idh FINanna lena mbaineann costais mh&eacute;adaithe arna n-eascairt as constaic&iacute; taraife agus neamhtharaife n&iacute;os airde. I dteannta luaineachta&iacute; i ngn&iacute;omha&iacute;ocht eacnama&iacute;och, f&eacute;adfaidh go mbeidh luaineacht i mbrab&uacute;is FIN agus sa chothroma&iacute;ocht choibhneasta idir tr&aacute;d&aacute;il earra&iacute; agus seirbh&iacute;s&iacute; i dt&eacute;arma&iacute; luacha agus m&eacute;id araon sa ghearrth&eacute;arma agus sa mhe&aacute;nt&eacute;arma. D&rsquo;fh&eacute;adfadh costais mh&eacute;adaitheacha t&aacute;irgthe agus costais d&aacute;ileach&aacute;in cur isteach faoi dheoidh ar bhrab&uacute;sacht FINanna in &Eacute;irinn sa mhe&aacute;nt&eacute;arma, agus bheadh tionchar di&uacute;ltach aige sin ar ioncam &oacute; ch&aacute;in chorpar&aacute;ide i gcompar&aacute;id le treochta&iacute; le d&eacute;ana&iacute;. In &eacute;agmais aon athruithe ar bheartas int&iacute;re agus idirn&aacute;isi&uacute;nta, &aacute;fach, is d&oacute;cha go dtiocfaidh m&eacute;ad&uacute; ar fh&aacute;ltais &oacute; ch&aacute;in chorpar&aacute;ide sa ghearrt&eacute;arma, fad a mheastar go rachaidh an t-easnamh bun&uacute;sach buis&eacute;id, lena n-eisiatar f&aacute;ltais &oacute; ch&aacute;in chorpar&aacute;ide, in olcas ar&iacute;s.</p><p>San fhadt&eacute;arma, t&aacute; an baol ann go mbeidh sreafa&iacute; n&iacute;os &iacute;sle infheist&iacute;ochta ann mar aon le slabhra&iacute; luacha athstrucht&uacute;raithe FIN go h&aacute;irithe m&aacute; bh&iacute;onn athruithe n&iacute;os leithne ar bheartas c&aacute;nach agus ar bheartas tionscla&iacute;och SA. D&rsquo;fh&eacute;adfadh s&eacute; sin bac a chur ar an bhf&aacute;s ar ghn&iacute;omha&iacute;ocht eacnama&iacute;och iarbh&iacute;r agus ar fhosta&iacute;ocht in &Eacute;irinn, rud a gh&eacute;ar&oacute;idh na d&uacute;shl&aacute;in don airgeadais poibl&iacute;. In <em>Alt S&iacute;nithe</em> a fhoils&iacute;tear in &eacute;ineacht leis an bh<em>Faisn&eacute;is</em> seo, tugann taighde &oacute;n bhfoireann le tuiscint go bhfuil an fh&eacute;idearthacht ann go mbeidh ioncam n&aacute;isi&uacute;nta na h&Eacute;ireann tuairim is 1 faoin gc&eacute;ad n&iacute;os &iacute;sle san fhadt&eacute;arma de bharr ch&oacute;ras taraife nua SA n&aacute; mar a bheadh i gceist murach sin.<sup><a href="#footnote-15" id="footnote-ref-16" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[15]</a></sup> T&aacute; difr&iacute;ochta&iacute; suntasacha ann ar fud na n-earn&aacute;lacha &eacute;ags&uacute;la &oacute; thaobh tionchar f&eacute;ideartha de, agus tugann samhlacha le tuiscint go mbeidh tortha&iacute; n&iacute;os di&uacute;lta&iacute; i gceist don earn&aacute;il ch&oacute;gais&iacute;ochta agus don earn&aacute;il ceimice&aacute;n, ar earn&aacute;lacha eachtracha iad don chuid is m&oacute;, fad gurb &iacute; an earn&aacute;il bia agus deochanna an ceann is suntasa&iacute; de na hearn&aacute;lacha int&iacute;re a nd&eacute;antar difear d&oacute;ibh.<sup><a href="#footnote-17" id="footnote-ref-17" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[16]</a></sup> Tugann an anail&iacute;s le tuiscint nach d&oacute;cha go n-eascr&oacute;idh laghd&uacute; suntasach ar infheist&iacute;ocht choigr&iacute;che reatha as na taraif&iacute; at&aacute; &aacute; dtabhairt isteach faoi l&aacute;thair, ach t&aacute; cailli&uacute;int ionchasach tharraingteacht na h&Eacute;ireann mar ard&aacute;n onnmhairi&uacute;ch&aacute;in do FDI nua &oacute; SA ina phr&iacute;omhriosca sa mhe&aacute;nt&eacute;arma.</p><p>Ar an ioml&aacute;n, t&aacute; an geilleagar int&iacute;re athl&eacute;imneach i gc&oacute;na&iacute; go dt&iacute; seo in 2025, sa mh&eacute;id go ndeimhn&iacute;onn sonra&iacute; Cuntas N&aacute;isi&uacute;nta &oacute;n bPr&iacute;omh-Oifig Staidrimh an tuairim a chuireamar in i&uacute;l san <em>Fhaisn&eacute;is R&aacute;ithi&uacute;il</em> i m&iacute; an Mh&aacute;rta maidir le fuinneamh an gheilleagair sa ch&eacute;ad leath den bhliain. T&aacute; an f&aacute;s ar fhosta&iacute;ocht os cionn 2 faoin gc&eacute;ad agus t&aacute; an d&iacute;fhosta&iacute;ocht s&aacute;ch &iacute;seal i gc&oacute;na&iacute;. T&aacute; br&uacute;nna boilscitheacha int&iacute;re faoi smacht i gc&oacute;na&iacute;, ach bh&iacute; an boilsci&uacute; ar phraghsanna bia s&aacute;ch ard le d&eacute;ana&iacute; mar gheall ar dh&aacute;la&iacute; sol&aacute;thair n&iacute;os g&eacute;ire i marga&iacute; mairteola na hEorpa (Bosca A). T&aacute; an f&aacute;s lean&uacute;nach measta ar fh&iacute;orioncaim indi&uacute;scartha thar thr&eacute;imhse na r&eacute;amhaisn&eacute;ise, i gcomhth&eacute;acs margadh saothair cobhsa&iacute;, ag tac&uacute; leis an bhf&aacute;s lean&uacute;nach measta ar chaiteachas tomhalt&oacute;ir&iacute;. </p><p>Ar a shon sin, t&aacute; comhartha&iacute; &aacute;irithe ann go bhfuil an fuinneamh ag maol&uacute;. T&aacute; laghd&uacute; tagtha ar an r&aacute;ta fol&uacute;ntais poist agus bh&iacute; f&aacute;s measartha ar ghn&iacute;omha&iacute;ocht eacnama&iacute;och sna hearn&aacute;lacha int&iacute;re den gheilleagar sa ch&eacute;ad leath de 2025. C&eacute; go bhf&eacute;afaidh go bhfuil an &eacute;iginnteacht maidir le beartas seachtrach ag cur isteach ar an ngeilleagar int&iacute;re, t&aacute; f&aacute;s ionchasach inbhuanaithe an gheilleagair &aacute; shrianadh ag bearna&iacute; i mbonneagar uisce, fuinnimh, iompair agus tith&iacute;ochta. Mar gheall ar an mbearna sheasmhach idir sol&aacute;thar seirbh&iacute;s&iacute; tith&iacute;ochta agus an t-&eacute;ileamh ar na seirbh&iacute;s&iacute; sin, t&aacute; praghsanna tith&iacute;ochta agus c&iacute;osanna ag m&eacute;ad&uacute; ar luas n&iacute;os tap&uacute;la n&aacute; ioncaim. &Oacute; tharla go bhfuil ionchais na dtomhalt&oacute;ir&iacute; &Eacute;ireannacha maidir le boilsci&uacute; forioml&aacute;n &aacute; m&uacute;nl&uacute; ag ionchais maidir le praghsanna tith&iacute;ochta, t&aacute; riosca&iacute; ann i gc&oacute;na&iacute; go mbeidh f&aacute;s eacnama&iacute;och &aacute; shrianadh ag forbairt&iacute; sa mhargadh tith&iacute;ochta agus, ina theannta sin, go mbeidh boilsci&uacute; n&iacute;os airde ann n&aacute; mar a bheadh murach sin (Bosca A). Ar an ioml&aacute;n, c&eacute; go bhfuil na riosca&iacute; a bhaineann leis an ionchas don fh&aacute;s eacnama&iacute;och n&iacute;os cothromaithe n&aacute; mar a bh&iacute; san <em>Fhaisn&eacute;is R&aacute;ithi&uacute;il</em> i m&iacute; an Mheithimh, is riosca&iacute; ar an taobh th&iacute;os iad i gc&oacute;na&iacute;.</p><p>Faoi l&aacute;thair, is ionann boilsci&uacute; sa limist&eacute;ar euro agus tuairim is 2 faoin gc&eacute;ad, is &eacute; sin an sprioc mhe&aacute;nt&eacute;armach, agus bh&iacute; meas&uacute;n&uacute; Chomhairle Rialaithe BCE ar an ionchas do bhoilsci&uacute; m&oacute;r&aacute;n gan athr&uacute; i ndiaidh a gcruinnithe i m&iacute; Mhe&aacute;n F&oacute;mhair i gcompar&aacute;id leis an gcruinni&uacute; socraithe beartais roimhe seo. I bhfianaise na sonra&iacute; at&aacute; ag teacht isteach, dhinimic an bhoilscithe bhun&uacute;saigh agus neart an tarchuir beartais airgeada&iacute;ochta, chinn an Chomhairle Rialaithe ag an gcruinni&uacute; sin go bhf&aacute;gfa&iacute; r&aacute;ta na saor&aacute;ide taisce gan athr&uacute; ag 2 faoin gc&eacute;ad.</p><p>Leis na d&uacute;shl&aacute;in ghearrth&eacute;armacha agus na hiarmhairt&iacute; fadt&eacute;armacha a bhaineann leis an timpeallacht athraitheach gheo-eacnama&iacute;och, leagtar b&eacute;im ar an ng&aacute; at&aacute; le tosa&iacute;ochta&iacute; soil&eacute;ire a bheith sa bheartas eacnama&iacute;och int&iacute;re. </p><p>Sa ghearrth&eacute;arma, n&iacute;l taca&iacute; fioscacha leathana riachtanach n&oacute; iomchu&iacute;, i bhfianaise chine&aacute;l na turrainge agus leochaileachta&iacute; bun&uacute;sacha san airgeadas poibl&iacute;. Ina ionad sin, is f&eacute;idir tac&uacute;, laistigh de pharaim&eacute;adair reatha na ngn&iacute;omhaireachta&iacute; St&aacute;it, le gn&oacute;lachta&iacute; d&uacute;chasacha onnmhairi&uacute;ch&aacute;in&mdash;at&aacute; ar na gn&oacute;tha&iacute; is rath&uacute;la agus is t&aacute;irgi&uacute;la in &Eacute;irinn cheana f&eacute;in&mdash;chun l&iacute;onra&iacute; agus marga&iacute; nua a fhorbairt. Go h&aacute;irithe, t&aacute; acmhainneacht neamhshaothraithe tr&aacute;d&aacute;la ag gn&oacute;tha&iacute; &Eacute;ireannacha le Ballst&aacute;it AE eile, lena bhf&eacute;adfa&iacute; tairbhe a bhaint as an margadh is m&oacute; ar domhan at&aacute; saor &oacute; tharaif&iacute; (BC&Eacute;, 2025). Ag obair i gcomhar le lucht d&eacute;anta beartais AE chun constaic&iacute; tr&aacute;d&aacute;la agus infheist&iacute;ochta laistigh den Aontas Eorpach a laghd&uacute;, f&eacute;adfar na deiseanna sin a thap&uacute;. &Aacute;ir&iacute;tear sa mh&eacute;id sin, dul chun cinn a dh&eacute;anamh maidir leis an Aontas Coigiltis agus Infheist&iacute;ochta (SIU), rud a chabhr&oacute;dh le teaghlaigh in &Eacute;irinn agus le teaghlaigh ar fud AE tortha&iacute; n&iacute;os airde a bhaint amach ar a gcuid coigilteas, fad a chuirf&iacute; rogha maoini&uacute;ch&aacute;in n&iacute;os fearr ar f&aacute;il do ghn&oacute;tha&iacute;. Sa bhreis air sin, f&eacute;adfar frithchuimilt&iacute; tr&aacute;d&aacute;la a laghd&uacute; tr&iacute; ch&oacute;rais &iacute;oca&iacute;ochta Eorpacha a l&aacute;nph&aacute;irti&uacute; n&iacute;os m&oacute;, rud a rachaidh chun tairbhe do ghn&oacute;tha&iacute; agus do theaghlaigh &Eacute;ireannacha. </p><p>Chun dul i ngleic leis na d&uacute;shl&aacute;in fhadt&eacute;armacha &oacute;n scoilt gheopholaiti&uacute;il, n&iacute; m&oacute;r dul i ngleic leis na srianta c&eacute;anna ar fh&aacute;s int&iacute;re a luaitear thuas tr&iacute; bhearna&iacute; i mbonneagar uisce, fuinnimh, iompair, agus tith&iacute;ochta a l&iacute;onadh. T&aacute; s&eacute; seo riachtanach chun tarraingteacht na h&Eacute;ireann don infheist&iacute;ocht dh&iacute;reach choigr&iacute;che a fheabhs&uacute; agus chun costais mhaireacht&aacute;la agus ghn&oacute; a shrianadh. F&eacute;adfar infheist&iacute;ocht phr&iacute;obh&aacute;ideach a phl&oacute;d&uacute; isteach le seachadadh &eacute;ifeacht&uacute;il bonneagair phoibl&iacute; arna bhaint amach n&iacute; hamh&aacute;in tr&iacute; chaiteachas m&eacute;adaithe caipitil ach tr&iacute; athch&oacute;irithe lena luasgh&eacute;ar&oacute;fa&iacute; aml&iacute;nte tionscadal freisin. Ar a shon sin, t&aacute; riosca&iacute; gearrth&eacute;armacha maidir le costais aonad saothair agus boilsci&uacute; n&iacute;os airde ag gabh&aacute;il leis an m&eacute;ad&uacute; riachtanach ar ghn&iacute;omha&iacute;ocht foirgn&iacute;ochta - earn&aacute;il ina bhfuil an t&aacute;irgi&uacute;lacht faoi bhun an mhe&aacute;in. D&rsquo;fhonn na riosca&iacute; seo a mhaol&uacute;, t&aacute; s&eacute; t&aacute;bhachtach go nd&eacute;anfa&iacute; caiteachas caipitil phoibl&iacute; a nascadh le modhanna nu&aacute;lacha seachadta agus sc&aacute;la i dtionscadail infheist&iacute;ochta a spreagadh. Leis na tionscnaimh seo, f&eacute;adfar tac&uacute; le t&aacute;irgi&uacute;lacht, f&eacute;adar br&uacute;nna boilscitheacha a mhaol&uacute;, agus f&eacute;adfar an bunt&aacute;iste eacnama&iacute;och &oacute; infheist&iacute;ocht phoibl&iacute; a uasmh&eacute;ad&uacute;. Ar bhonn n&iacute;os leithne, cuirfear le f&aacute;s t&aacute;irgi&uacute;lachta n&iacute;os airde freisin tr&iacute; dhinimiceas gn&oacute; n&iacute;os fearr a &eacute;asc&uacute;, mar aon le hathdh&aacute;ileadh caipitil n&iacute;os &eacute;ifeacht&uacute;la, de mar a thiocfaidh gn&oacute;lachta&iacute; &oacute;ga chun cinn.</p><p>T&aacute; an g&aacute; go laghd&oacute;fa&iacute; riosca&iacute; don airgeadais poibl&iacute; tar &eacute;is &eacute;ir&iacute; n&iacute;os g&eacute;ire, ar riosca&iacute; iad a eascra&iacute;onn as bonn c&aacute;nach at&aacute; r&oacute;-ch&uacute;ng, i bhfianaise an sple&aacute;chais ar fh&aacute;ltais &oacute; ch&aacute;in chorpar&aacute;ide &oacute; l&iacute;on beag FIN a fh&eacute;adfaidh a bheith n&iacute;os leochail&iacute; mar gheall ar an scoilt gheo-eacnama&iacute;och. Sa <a href="https://www.gov.ie/en/commission-on-taxation-and-welfare/publications/report-of-the-commission-on-taxation-and-welfare/">Tuarasc&aacute;il &oacute;n gCoimisi&uacute;n um Ch&aacute;nachas agus Leas</a> in 2022, cuireadh roghanna inmharthana i l&aacute;thair maidir le fairsinge an bhoinn c&aacute;nach a choime&aacute;d ar bun agus a leathn&uacute; ar fud faoiseamh c&aacute;nach, c&aacute;nacha maoine agus tomhaltais agus rann&iacute;oca&iacute;ochta&iacute; &aacute;rachais sh&oacute;isialta, fad a chaomhn&oacute;far, nuair is iomchu&iacute;, m&eacute;id an dul chun cinn sa ch&oacute;ras c&aacute;nach ioncaim phearsanta.<sup><a href="#footnote-18" id="footnote-ref-18" data-sf-ec-immutable="">[17]</a></sup> Le bun&uacute; an Chiste um &Eacute;ire sa Todhcha&iacute; agus an Chiste um Bonneagar, Aer&aacute;id agus an D&uacute;lra, n&iacute;ltear ag dul i ngleic ach go p&aacute;irteach le br&uacute;nna breise caiteachais phoibl&iacute; at&aacute; ar eolas, ar br&uacute;nna iad a thiocfaidh chun cinn sna 2030id&iacute; agus ina dhiaidh sin. T&aacute; an tiomantas do bhonn inchreidte fioscach lena n-&aacute;irith&iacute;tear f&aacute;s inbhuanaithe ar ghlanchaiteachas rialtais r&iacute;th&aacute;bhachtach i gc&oacute;na&iacute;. Ar an gcaoi sin, bhun&oacute;fa&iacute; beartas fioscach &eacute;ifeachtach fritimthriallach, rud a chumas&oacute;dh don airgeadas poibl&iacute; tac&uacute; leis an ngeilleagar de r&eacute;ir mar is g&aacute;, agus a neart&oacute;dh an t-airgeadas poibl&iacute; le imeacht ama. Is g&aacute; go mbeidh bonn inmharthana tiomsaithe ioncaim ag gabh&aacute;il le f&aacute;s ar chaiteachas rialtais, go h&aacute;irithe caiteachas reatha athfhillteach, i bhfianaise na leochaileachta&iacute; arna n-eascairt as r&oacute;chaiteachas seasmhach agus easnaimh bhun&uacute;sacha buis&eacute;id. Sa bhreis air sin, t&aacute; s&eacute; r&iacute;th&aacute;bhachtach go leathn&oacute;far an bonn c&aacute;nach d&rsquo;fhonn an acmhainn fhioscach agus eacnama&iacute;och a chruth&uacute; chun infheist&iacute;ocht i gcaipiteal poibl&iacute; a mh&eacute;ad&uacute; mar at&aacute; beartaithe sa Phlean Forbartha N&aacute;isi&uacute;nta agus chun na costais mh&eacute;adaitheacha at&aacute; ag teacht chun cinn a chl&uacute;dach d&rsquo;fhonn seirbh&iacute;s&iacute; poibl&iacute; a choime&aacute;d ar bun ag a leibh&eacute;il reatha.</p>
    
   

    
